# Business Model and Revenue-Stream Definitions

**Industrial Automation & Robotics Finance Portfolio | Week 1, Day 4**

*Aligned to the Day 3 company profile for Mirdain Robotics Systems Ltd (fictional): £9.0m FY2025 revenue, 27.0% gross margin, about £0.7m EBITDA, 32 employees. Stream margins and cost splits are Day 4 allocations that reconcile to those Day 3 totals, and will be locked in the Day 6 assumptions register.*

## 1. Purpose

This document defines how Mirdain Robotics Systems Ltd (fictional) makes money. It sets out the four revenue streams, how each is priced, recognised and forecast, and how they connect. Every figure ties back to the Day 3 profile. It is the reference for the Day 5 historical dataset, the Day 9 revenue build, the Day 10 COGS model and the Day 29 Power BI revenue measures. Each stream is defined so that no transaction is counted twice.

## 2. Business model in one paragraph

Mirdain is a Coventry-based automation integrator (founded 2009, privately owned) that designs, supplies and installs robotic production cells, then supports them through service, spares and predictive maintenance. About 60% of FY2025 revenue (£5.4m of £9.0m) is lumpy, fixed-price project work, and about 40% is shorter-cycle or recurring. Projects win the customer, and the aftermarket steadies earnings, so growing the service base is a strategic aim. Assembly, installation and field service are outsourced, which keeps fixed costs low but makes margin depend on subcontractor rates and availability.

## 3. The four revenue streams

### 3.1 Robotics Systems Projects

- **What it is:** Design, supply and commissioning of complete robotic production cells, including robots, tooling, safety systems, controls and machine vision.
- **FY2025 size:** About 60% of revenue (£5.4m), from about 12 projects at an average of about £450k.
- **Customers:** Automotive Tier 1/2 suppliers (largest segment), food, beverage and packaging, logistics and warehousing, and general manufacturing. Project sizes range from about £100k to £1.2m by segment.
- **Contract and billing:** Fixed price, billed on milestones: 30% on order, 20% at design approval, 30% at delivery to site, 15% at acceptance, and 5% retention released at the end of the 12-month warranty. Customer payment terms are 30 to 60 days (automotive 60 days).
- **Duration:** 6 to 12 months from contract award to handover, after 2 to 4 months of enquiry and quotation.
- **Revenue recognition:** Over time, using cost-to-cost percentage of completion. Billing differs from revenue, creating contract assets or deferred income.
- **Gross margin:** 23% (£1.24m).
- **Main costs (£m):** Equipment and components 3.12, subcontracted assembly and installation 0.56, engineering labour 0.45, other 0.03.
- **Key risks:** Estimating error and scope change on fixed-price work, EUR and JPY purchase costs against GBP prices, subcontractor cost and availability, installation delays and rework.
- **Cash profile:** Supplier payments often fall due before the delivery milestone, so peak funding need sits between procurement and installation.
- **Model forecast driver:** Projects per year × average contract value × delivery curve over 6 to 12 months.

### 3.2 Engineering & Integration

- **What it is:** Chargeable engineering sold on its own: feasibility studies, cell design, robot programming, AI and vision integration, and upgrades to existing lines.
- **Boundary with Projects:** Engineering inside a turnkey project contract is Projects revenue. This stream is only work contracted separately.
- **FY2025 size:** About 15% of revenue (£1.35m).
- **Customers:** Mainly general manufacturing, including many first-time automation buyers, plus installed-base customers. Studies often lead into a full project.
- **Contract and billing:** Time and materials, or fixed-fee work packages, billed on delivery or milestones.
- **Revenue recognition:** As work is delivered (time and materials) or over time (fixed fee).
- **Gross margin:** 34% (£0.46m), the highest of the four streams because it is mostly engineering labour with limited equipment.
- **Main costs (£m):** Engineering labour 0.45, subcontracted programming and specialist support 0.44.
- **Key risks:** Competes with Projects for the same 20 engineers, underpriced fixed-fee scopes, and low utilisation.
- **Cash profile:** Short billing cycles and low working-capital need.
- **Model forecast driver:** Billable engineering days × utilisation × day rate (Day 11), plus the rate at which studies convert into projects.

### 3.3 Service & Maintenance

- **What it is:** Planned maintenance, breakdown cover, remote monitoring and AI-enabled predictive maintenance, delivered with subcontracted field-service partners.
- **FY2025 size:** About 15% of revenue (£1.35m).
- **Customers:** Mostly the installed base. About 70% of service and spares revenue comes from customers who previously bought a Mirdain project.
- **Contract and billing:** Mainly annual contracts billed in advance or quarterly, giving recurring and more predictable revenue.
- **Revenue recognition:** Straight-line over the contract term, with advance billing held as deferred income.
- **Gross margin:** 32% (£0.43m). Because partners do the field work, this is a resale margin and sits below what self-delivered labour would earn.
- **Main costs (£m):** Field-service partners 0.50, in-house engineering for remote monitoring and predictive maintenance 0.30, parts used 0.10, other 0.02.
- **Key risks:** Partner availability and rates, churn once the 12-month warranty ends, and service-level commitments that outrun partner capacity.
- **Cash profile:** Favourable when billed in advance.
- **Model forecast driver:** Opening contracts plus new contracts attached to completed projects (after warranty) less churn, × average annual contract value.

### 3.4 Spare Parts

- **What it is:** Supply of robot spares, grippers, sensors, cameras and consumables to installed-base customers.
- **Boundary with Service:** Parts used under a service contract are costed in Service. This stream is parts sold separately.
- **FY2025 size:** About 10% of revenue (£0.9m).
- **Customers:** Installed-base customers, mostly buyers of an earlier Mirdain project.
- **Contract and billing:** Transactional orders priced at a resale margin, with volume growing as the installed base grows.
- **Revenue recognition:** At a point in time, on delivery.
- **Gross margin:** 33% (£0.30m), a resale margin.
- **Main costs (£m):** Purchase cost of parts 0.56, freight and handling 0.04.
- **Key risks:** EUR and JPY purchase costs, supplier lead times, and customers buying directly from the manufacturer.
- **Cash profile:** Inventory is limited because stock is bought against customer orders, but supplier payments can fall due before customer receipts.
- **Model forecast driver:** Installed base size × annual spares spend rate.

